// Regression: `where t is *t` (and equivalents like `where t is
// pointer[t]` after type-alias expansion) is unsatisfiable
// because no concrete type T can equal *T -- the occurs check
// makes the constraint recursive.  The checker rejects the
// declaration at parse-time instead of silently producing a fn
// no caller can satisfy (which surfaced earlier as the misleading
// "undefined: <fn>" error).

fn bad[t]() t where t is *t = return nil as t

fn main() i64 = return 0
